## Session Restoration Across Requests

The hosted browser chat endpoint uses the **AG-UI protocol** (Agent-Generated UI) over Server-Sent Events. Session persistence is handled by a three-part pipeline:

### 1. Thread ID as the Conversation Key

Every incoming AG-UI request carries a `thread_id` in its body (`RunAgentInput.ThreadId`). The endpoint treats this as the conversation identifier:

```csharp
// AGUIEndpointRouteBuilderExtensions.cs:118
var threadId = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(input.ThreadId)
    ? Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N") : input.ThreadId;
```

If the browser sends no `thread_id`, a new one is generated. Otherwise, the same `thread_id` is used to look up the existing session.

### 2. `AIHostAgent` + `AgentSessionStore`

The endpoint wraps the agent in an `AIHostAgent`, which delegates session I/O to an `AgentSessionStore`:

```csharp
// AGUIEndpointRouteBuilderExtensions.cs:85-86
var agentSessionStore = endpoints.ServiceProvider
    .GetKeyedService<AgentSessionStore>(aiAgent.Name);
var hostAgent = new AIHostAgent(aiAgent,
    agentSessionStore ?? new NoopAgentSessionStore());
```

On each request, the session is restored (or created fresh) by `thread_id`:

```csharp
// AGUIEndpointRouteBuilderExtensions.cs:119
var session = await hostAgent
    .GetOrCreateSessionAsync(threadId, cancellationToken);
```

`GetOrCreateSessionAsync` calls into `AgentSessionStore.GetSessionAsync(agent, conversationId)`. The store serializes/deserializes the session as JSON:

```csharp
// InMemoryAgentSessionStore.cs:40-49
public override async ValueTask<AgentSession> GetSessionAsync(
    AIAgent agent, string conversationId, ...)
{
    var key = GetKey(conversationId, agent.Id);
    JsonElement? sessionContent = this._threads
        .TryGetValue(key, out var existingSession) ? existingSession : null;

    return sessionContent switch
    {
        null => await agent.CreateSessionAsync(cancellationToken),
        _    => await agent.DeserializeSessionAsync(sessionContent.Value, ...)
    };
}
```

### 3. Session Saved After Streaming Completes

The session is **not** saved mid-stream. Instead, the event stream is wrapped so that the save happens only after all SSE events have been yielded:

```csharp
// AGUIEndpointRouteBuilderExtensions.cs:143-156
private static async IAsyncEnumerable<BaseEvent>
    SaveSessionAfterStreamingAsync(
        IAsyncEnumerable<BaseEvent> events,
        AIHostAgent hostAgent, string threadId,
        AgentSession session, ...)
{
    await foreach (BaseEvent evt in events)
        yield return evt;

    // Session saved only after the stream completes
    await hostAgent.SaveSessionAsync(threadId, session, ...);
}
```

This means the session accumulates all messages and state changes during the streaming run, then persists atomically at the end.

---

## Separating Browser-Side Tools from Server-Side Tools

When the LLM produces tool calls, some tools are meant to execute on the **server** (e.g., database access, internal APIs) and others on the **browser/client** (e.g., display a map, show a generative UI component). The framework prevents server tool calls from leaking into the browser stream.

### The Filter: `FilterServerToolsFromMixedToolInvocationsAsync`

The streaming pipeline inserts a filter between the agent's raw `ChatResponseUpdate` stream and the AG-UI event converter:

```csharp
// AGUIEndpointRouteBuilderExtensions.cs:122-133
var events = hostAgent.RunStreamingAsync(messages, session, runOptions, ...)
    .AsChatResponseUpdatesAsync()
    .FilterServerToolsFromMixedToolInvocationsAsync(clientTools, ...)
    .AsAGUIEventStreamAsync(threadId, input.RunId, ...);
```

The filter logic (`AGUIChatResponseUpdateStreamExtensions.cs`) works as follows:

```csharp
public static async IAsyncEnumerable<ChatResponseUpdate>
    FilterServerToolsFromMixedToolInvocationsAsync(
        this IAsyncEnumerable<ChatResponseUpdate> updates,
        List<AITool>? clientTools, ...)
{
    // Build a set of client tool names
    var set = new HashSet<string>(clientTools!);

    await foreach (var update in updates)
    {
        if (update.FinishReason == ChatFinishReason.ToolCalls)
        {
            // Check if this update contains BOTH client and server tool calls
            var containsClientTools = false;
            var containsServerTools = false;
            foreach (var content in update.Contents)
            {
                if (content is FunctionCallContent fcc)
                {
                    containsClientTools |= set.Contains(fcc.Name);
                    containsServerTools |= !set.Contains(fcc.Name);
                }
            }

            if (containsClientTools && containsServerTools)
            {
                // Mixed: strip server tool calls, keep only client ones
                var newContents = new List<AIContent>();
                foreach (var content in update.Contents)
                {
                    if (content is not FunctionCallContent fcc ||
                        set.Contains(fcc.Name))
                    {
                        newContents.Add(content);
                    }
                }
                yield return new ChatResponseUpdate(update.Role, newContents) { ... };
            }
            else
            {
                yield return update;  // Pure client or pure server — pass through
            }
        }
        else
        {
            yield return update;  // Non-tool-call updates pass through unchanged
        }
    }
}
```

### How the Separation Works End-to-End

| Step | What happens | Where |
|------|-------------|-------|
| **1. Client declares tools** | The browser sends `tools` in the AG-UI request body — these are the *client-side* tools it can execute | `RunAgentInput.Tools` |
| **2. Server runs the agent** | The LLM sees both server tools (registered on the agent) and client tools (passed via `ChatOptions.Tools`). It may emit tool calls for either or both | `hostAgent.RunStreamingAsync(...)` |
| **3. Server executes its own tools** | Server-side tool calls are executed server-side; their results are fed back to the LLM | Inside the agent's tool invocation pipeline |
| **4. Stream is filtered** | When the response update contains `FinishReason == ToolCalls`, the filter checks each `FunctionCallContent`: if the tool name is in the client's tool set, it stays; otherwise it is stripped | `FilterServerToolsFromMixedToolInvocationsAsync` |
| **5. Browser receives only its tool calls** | The SSE stream contains only client-side tool calls, which the browser executes and sends back as results | `AGUIServerSentEventsResult` → browser |

The key insight is that **server tools are executed entirely on the server** and their results never appear in the SSE stream to the browser. Only the tool calls the browser is registered to handle are sent downstream. When a single LLM turn produces both kinds of calls (a "mixed" invocation), the filter surgically removes the server-side ones from that update before it reaches the wire.

